<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Sinope</h1><p class="st-meta">631 BCE – 183 BCE (approx.) · capital Sinope</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Milesian colony with the best harbour on the southern Black Sea, birthplace of Diogenes the Cynic and later capital of the Kingdom of Pontus.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/miletus">Miletus</a> founded Sinope on a peninsula on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>; ancient sources give dates in the 8th and 7th centuries BCE, and the register follows c. 631 BCE. Its sheltered harbour made it the main Greek port of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Sinope founded colonies of its own, including <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/trapezus">Trapezus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerasus">Cerasus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotyora">Cotyora</a>, and traded tuna, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and red ochre called Sinopic earth. It was under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> and later independent. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> expedition of c. 437 BCE installed Athenian settlers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diogenes-the-cynic">Diogenes the Cynic</a> was born there c. 412 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharnaces-i-of-pontus">Pharnaces I</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus</a> captured Sinope in 183 BCE and made it his capital; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI</a> was born there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> took it in 70 BCE. It is the modern Turkish town of Sinop.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 631 BCE – 183 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V6